// compute_shl and mul_word are the two operator computations that need a
// `word` intermediate (../core.id's fold_word turns the result back into a
// folded int, or "" when it does not fit one).
//
// A shift count of 32 or more is exactly where int and word disagree: id's
// shift is defined for every count (docs/SPEC.md 2.3), but "0 gives up"
// only holds at width 32 -- at width 64 the same count is still a real
// shift. So a count that reaches 32 is left unfolded, and below that,
// shl_word (../link_e.id) computes the shift in 64 bits and fold_word only
// keeps it if it never needed the bits an int does not have.

compute_shl(int a, int b) {
  string ret_s = "";
  if(b < 32) {
    word shifted = shl_word(a, b);
    ret_s = fold_word(shifted);
  }
} return string ret_s;

// Neither operand exceeds INT_MAX, so their product cannot exceed
// INT_MAX * INT_MAX, well inside what a 64-bit word holds without wrapping.
mul_word(int a, int b) {
  word aw = a;
  word bw = b;
  word prod = aw * bw;
} return word prod;
